Scenerio 
A cyber-attack took place against an organization where phishing was done. The employees received emails that appeared to come from a reputable bank.
The email contained malicious links which were clicked by the victim. Clicking on the link, the attacker then attempted to perform unauthorized access to the system.
Due to the above activity, there were multiple failed login attempts and triggers generated within the SIEM platform.
The SOC analyst identified the event and executed the necessary actions.

DETECTION PHASE

This type of attack was detected using various forms of security monitoring tools.
A peculiar phishing mail was observed by the user and reported as unusual.
This email consisted of an odd link that did not pertain to the domain.
Upon clicking on the link, various login attempts were observed to have failed.
Various alerts were generated from the SIEM system (Splunk) based on the unusual login attempts.
Based on these alerts, the possibility of accessing the network existed.
An analysis by the SOC analyst confirmed that there was some security threat.

Containment Phase
After confirming that a phishing attack had been carried out, several steps were taken to stop further damage.
The email containing the phishing scam was flagged as malicious and deleted from the inbox of the user (simulation)
The domain name of the email address used to conduct the phishing scam was blacklisted
The malicious URL was detected, and access to it was stopped (simulation)
The compromised user account was disabled
Steps were taken to deny access to any suspicious IP addresses
All users were advised not to respond to such emails

ERADICATION PHASE
Once the phishing attack was contained, measures were taken to fully eradicate all vestiges of the attack from the system and guard against recurrence.
Phishing emails received were entirely wiped out from the organizational email system (simulation)
Malicious files and scripts downloaded from the phishing link were located and deleted (simulation)
The infected computer was thoroughly searched for malware or any other suspicious activity
The password of the compromised account was changed to secure the system
Malicious processes running on the infected computer were stopped
Security patches and updates were installed to shield the system from attacks
These were key steps taken to fully eliminate the threat from the system.

RECOVERY PHASE 
Having successfully dealt with the phishing attack, measures were put in place to fix the affected systems to enable their proper functionality.
The compromised user account was activated through password recovery by setting a stronger password.
The multi-factor authentication method was recommended and activated to protect the system against possible future attacks.
The compromised system was under constant monitoring for signs of any unusual activity.
Services and applications on the system were brought back to their normal status.
System logs were checked again for the sake of verifying that the attack was indeed eliminated.
Any backed-up data on the system that was affected was also recovered.
Finally, security awareness guidelines were offered to the user in order to protect him from such an attack in the future.

Lessons Learned
Phishing emails remain a major entry point for attackers
Users must verify email domains before clicking links
Early detection through SIEM alerts is critical
Strong password policies and MFA improve security
Security awareness training is essential for prevention

